WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:04.160
Catastrophic earthquake scenarios

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:07.190
have played out on the
silver screen for decades,

00:00:07.190 --> 00:00:10.420
terrifying viewers with quakes
that can collapse skyscrapers

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:12.850
or topple entire cities.

00:00:12.850 --> 00:00:16.713
Here's what will happen if the
big one hits the West Coast.

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:22.510
On July 4, 2019, Ridgecrest, California,

00:00:22.510 --> 00:00:25.550
was hit with a 6.4 magnitude earthquake

00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:28.860
and then a 7.1 just one day later.

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:32.110
But neither of these compare
to the long-awaited big one,

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:34.060
which scientists predict will eventually

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:36.010
rattle the golden coast.

00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:39.010
But when it hits, what will
that actually look like?

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:40.810
Here's what experts say could happen

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:44.770
in the seconds, hours, and
days after the big one.

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:48.320
While experts can't know
exactly when a quake will occur,

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:51.320
they have a pretty good idea of where.

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:54.630
California is located in
a hot zone of fault lines,

00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:58.240
the most notorious of them
the San Andreas Fault.

00:00:58.240 --> 00:00:59.880
John Vidale: You know, here
in California you have dangers

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:02.020
from a number of different
kinds of earthquakes.

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:03.780
The major danger is from the earthquakes

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:06.020
on the San Andreas Fault system.

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:07.980
Narrator: On average,
the San Andreas Fault

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:10.630
ruptures every 150 years.

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:13.180
The southern parts of the
fault have remained inactive

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:15.480
for over 200 years.

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:18.089
Vidale: We haven't had a big earthquake in

00:01:18.089 --> 00:01:21.898
Southern California really since 1857.

00:01:21.898 --> 00:01:25.040
Narrator: In other words, we're
overdue for a major shake.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:28.060
According to a 2008 federal report,

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:32.090
the most likely scenario
is a 7.8 magnitude quake

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:34.560
that would rupture a 200-mile stretch

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:37.070
along the southernmost part of the fault.

00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:40.124
Vidale: It's basically moving
the ground several yards

00:01:40.124 --> 00:01:43.420
over an area of 50 square miles.

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:46.470
So the power of a magnitude 7.8 earthquake

00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:48.450
is probably close to the power

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:50.740
used in the whole state for a year.

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:54.300
Basically something that
we as a civilization

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:58.633
have trouble creating, short
of, like, a nuclear explosion.

00:02:00.470 --> 00:02:02.810
Narrator: If you are near the
epicenter of the earthquake,

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:05.380
it will be nearly impossible to stand.

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:07.680
Vidale: People have this
idea of running out of bed,

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:10.110
out of their buildings,
and that's a terrible idea,

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:12.630
because a lot of what we
see in earthquakes is people

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:16.410
with broken legs and people
who've run through glass.

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:18.480
The best thing to do, like we always say,

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:20.280
is duck, cover, and hold.

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:22.240
Get under some piece of furniture.

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:26.050
The main point is to
protect your head and chest.

00:02:26.050 --> 00:02:28.410
Narrator: During and immediately
following the shaking,

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:30.440
buildings could collapse.

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:32.120
John Wallace: The number of
buildings that were constructed

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:35.050
before about 1980 is really significant,

00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:36.190
and most of these buildings

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:39.810
are very vulnerable to
damage and collapse.

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:41.230
Narrator: In this time-lapse video,

00:02:41.230 --> 00:02:43.680
you can see how building
components would hold up

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:46.070
in a high-magnitude earthquake.

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:47.740
Wallace: 'Cause the San
Andreas will produce

00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:50.070
the kind of long-period shaking

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:53.210
which would be very damaging
to very tall buildings,

00:02:53.210 --> 00:02:56.240
say, in downtown LA, and Century City,

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:58.160
and Long Beach, and so forth.

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:01.050
Older steel buildings,
the connections in them

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:03.030
have not necessarily been designed

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:04.830
to withstand the maximum forces

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:06.997
that actually can be generated.

00:03:06.997 --> 00:03:09.720
Narrator: Unreinforced
structures are the least stable,

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:12.253
but even buildings up
to code could crumble.

00:03:13.310 --> 00:03:14.895
John Stewart: The building code, with its

00:03:14.895 --> 00:03:18.780
minimum requirements, does
not ensure that the building

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:20.970
will be serviceable after an earthquake.

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:23.600
It's intended to not kill anybody.

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:26.520
There's a sense that if
it's modern, code-designed,

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:29.510
it's earthquake-proof and
everything should be great,

00:03:29.510 --> 00:03:31.060
but that's not the reality.

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:32.420
Narrator: Five steel high-rises

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:35.260
could collapse completely, while 10 others

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:37.943
will be red-tagged, or unsafe to enter.

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:41.900
And, no, the quake would
not cause a tsunami,

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:44.050
despite what movies
would have you believe.

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:47.560
Vidale: To trigger a tsunami,
it takes an earthquake

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:49.890
that moves the ocean floor,

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:52.650
and most of the San Andreas is on land,

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:55.630
so there would be a little
bit of waves generated

00:03:55.630 --> 00:03:57.900
from a San Andreas earthquake,

00:03:57.900 --> 00:03:59.700
but nothing that would be dangerous.

00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:04.070
Narrator: The quake could
kill about 1,800 people

00:04:04.070 --> 00:04:07.590
and leave 50,000 or more with injuries.

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:09.600
While people could die from falling debris

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:11.210
and collapsed structures,

00:04:11.210 --> 00:04:13.773
the highest death toll
would be from fires.

00:04:14.810 --> 00:04:16.430
Vidale: Historically, they biggest hazard

00:04:16.430 --> 00:04:18.725
from earthquakes has been fire.

00:04:18.725 --> 00:04:22.500
In the 1906 earthquake there
were 3,000 or 4,000 people

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:25.060
who were just caught in that wave of fire

00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:26.460
that swept through the city.

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:30.420
Narrator: The aftermath of the big one

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:34.425
will wreak havoc on
infrastructure and the economy.

00:04:34.425 --> 00:04:37.230
Scott Brandenberg: Below our
streets and our buildings

00:04:37.230 --> 00:04:40.110
is this really complicated
network of infrastructure

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:43.100
that could be damaged,
and a lot of the things

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:44.825
we take for granted every day

00:04:44.825 --> 00:04:46.416
won't be available anymore, right?

00:04:46.416 --> 00:04:48.424
Like water, electricity,

00:04:48.424 --> 00:04:50.610
being able to drive
where you need to drive.

00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:55.140
Narrator: Parts of the San Andreas Fault

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:58.640
intersect with 39 gas and oil pipelines.

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:01.040
This could rupture
high-pressure gas lines,

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:02.630
releasing gas into the air

00:05:02.630 --> 00:05:05.603
and igniting potentially
deadly explosions.

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:09.430
Stewart: So, if you have
natural-gas lines that rupture,

00:05:09.430 --> 00:05:11.910
that's how you can get
fire and explosions.

00:05:11.910 --> 00:05:13.610
Narrator: And after the fires burn out,

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:16.210
one of the biggest concerns
in a major earthquake

00:05:16.210 --> 00:05:18.310
is access to fresh water.

00:05:18.310 --> 00:05:20.270
The major aqueduct networks

00:05:20.270 --> 00:05:22.850
that pump water into Southern California

00:05:22.850 --> 00:05:24.790
all cross the San Andreas Fault

00:05:24.790 --> 00:05:27.520
and could be seriously damaged.

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:30.010
Stewart: So we would be
without the lifelines

00:05:30.010 --> 00:05:32.230
that bring in imported
water to the region.

00:05:32.230 --> 00:05:33.670
They cross through tunnels,

00:05:33.670 --> 00:05:36.140
cross through aqueducts near the surface.

00:05:36.140 --> 00:05:37.440
All of these would be ruptured,

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:40.660
and so we would be losing
60% of our water supply.

00:05:40.660 --> 00:05:42.480
Many of these distribution lines for water

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:45.950
are near sewer lines,
which would also be broken,

00:05:45.950 --> 00:05:49.170
so now you have a situation
where contaminants

00:05:49.170 --> 00:05:51.380
are potentially getting
into the water supply.

00:05:51.380 --> 00:05:52.870
Narrator: Experts say you should keep

00:05:52.870 --> 00:05:56.540
at least a two-week supply
of water in your home.

00:05:56.540 --> 00:05:59.290
As the ground shakes and sediments shift,

00:05:59.290 --> 00:06:01.890
there will be landslides
throughout Ventura

00:06:01.890 --> 00:06:03.603
and Western Los Angeles County.

00:06:04.630 --> 00:06:06.700
Brandenberg: There could
be thousands of landslides.

00:06:06.700 --> 00:06:08.820
There have been earthquakes
that have produced thousands.

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:13.023
Landslides definitely can cause
fatalities, property damage.

00:06:13.970 --> 00:06:16.500
We have a lot of people who
live up in the hills. Right?

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:19.330
So that's the location
where you would be likely

00:06:19.330 --> 00:06:22.650
to see landslides affecting people.

00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:24.210
Narrator: And finally, the big one

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:27.010
will severely impact the economy.

00:06:27.010 --> 00:06:30.450
Major transportation networks,
like highways and railways,

00:06:30.450 --> 00:06:34.140
could be unusable for
weeks and even months.

00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:37.023
Brandenberg: Some bridges
may not be passable

00:06:37.023 --> 00:06:38.173
after an earthquake.

00:06:38.173 --> 00:06:40.490
We've had bridges collapse
during past earthquakes.

00:06:40.490 --> 00:06:41.771
Stewart: You might start seeing

00:06:41.771 --> 00:06:45.047
key industries leave, population loss,

00:06:45.047 --> 00:06:46.446
and this could have, you know,

00:06:46.446 --> 00:06:48.854
devastating long-term
impacts for the region.

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:56.000
Narrator: The estimated financial cost

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:59.250
of the big one is a whopping $200 billion,

00:06:59.250 --> 00:07:02.220
with $33 billion in building damages

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:05.950
and $50 billion in lost economic activity.

00:07:05.950 --> 00:07:08.720
This all sounds pretty
bad, but keep in mind

00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:11.470
that this is based off
of a worst-case scenario.

00:07:11.470 --> 00:07:13.380
The true impact of a major earthquake

00:07:13.380 --> 00:07:16.780
is based on a range of unknowable factors.

00:07:16.780 --> 00:07:19.110
Also, smaller earthquakes on faults

00:07:19.110 --> 00:07:21.780
directly beneath major population centers

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:23.798
are a serious concern.

00:07:23.798 --> 00:07:25.073
Vidale: But the worst-case earthquakes

00:07:25.073 --> 00:07:26.232
are hard to predict.

00:07:26.232 --> 00:07:29.533
You know, that earthquake
in Japan in 2011,

00:07:30.770 --> 00:07:33.450
their cost almost entirely came

00:07:33.450 --> 00:07:36.880
because their nuclear
power plant melted down.

00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:38.600
It's very hard to
predict what's gonna fail

00:07:38.600 --> 00:07:40.040
in a big earthquake.

00:07:40.040 --> 00:07:41.980
Narrator: So, how can Californians

00:07:41.980 --> 00:07:43.610
prepare for the big one?

00:07:43.610 --> 00:07:45.295
Brandenberg: Really have a plan in place.

00:07:45.295 --> 00:07:46.253
You know, where are you going to meet?

00:07:46.253 --> 00:07:47.680
What are you going to do?

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:48.670
Have water ready.

00:07:48.670 --> 00:07:50.910
I have a 55-gallon drum full of water.

00:07:50.910 --> 00:07:52.440
There's some chemical additive I put in it

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:54.440
so it's potable for five years.

00:07:54.440 --> 00:07:56.440
Fifty-five gallons is the
right amount for my...

00:07:56.440 --> 00:07:58.040
I have a family of four.

00:07:58.040 --> 00:08:00.000
That'll last us for two weeks.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:00.870
Canned food.

00:08:00.870 --> 00:08:02.590
You know, you have to be ready.

00:08:02.590 --> 00:08:04.010
I would say it's best just to plan

00:08:04.010 --> 00:08:06.220
to stay sort of where you are.

00:08:06.220 --> 00:08:08.880
Getting out of LA is bad enough

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:10.090
without an earthquake, right?

00:08:10.090 --> 00:08:11.830
Traffic's already terrible.

00:08:11.830 --> 00:08:13.900
If roads are closed and people
are all trying to leave,

00:08:13.900 --> 00:08:15.508
it's gonna really be bad.

